%I A386859 #25 Nov 12 2026 16:48:07
%S A386859 32,13504,13141792,23076152576,64068865333248
%N A386859 Sum of the number of steps taken by all n-state Turing machines that halt.
%C A386859 max(A004147(n), A060843(n)) <= a(n) <= A004147(n)*A060843(n).
%C A386859 This sequence is noncomputable, because its computability would allow to solve the undecidable halting problem by letting a given n-state Turing machine run for up to a(n) steps.
%C A386859 Turing machines are n-state, 2-symbol, with movement in {left, right}.
%C A386859 a(5) was computed by walking the tree of partial machines from a blank tape, weighting each halting leaf by the number of full machines it stands for, and running every other branch until it repeats exactly, repeats shifted between record cells, is ruled out by backward reasoning or an n-gram closed position set, or reaches A060843(5) = 47176870 steps. The same walk gives A004147(5) = 26751695615616. A second program with no non-halting deciders gives the same machines and steps up to step 2000, and A004147(5) minus its count equals the weight of the 333 halting branches past step 2000, each rerun from blank. - _Joshua Trommel_, Oct 28 2026
%H A386859 Joshua Trommel, <a href="https://github.com/nulljosh/noema/blob/main/cs/halters.c">C program</a> and <a href="https://github.com/nulljosh/noema/blob/main/cs/halters-5.json">5-state output</a>.
%e A386859 a(1) = 32, because there are 32 1-state Turing machines that halt after 1 step each.
%e A386859 a(2) = 13504 = 1*6912 + 2*2304 + 3*384 + 4*128 + 5*16 + 6*40, the total steps from 9784 halting 2-state Turing machines, grouped by runtime.
%Y A386859 Cf. A004147, A060843.
%K A386859 nonn,hard,more,changed
%O A386859 1,1
%A A386859 _Carlos Moya Garcia_, Aug 05 2025
%E A386859 a(5) from _Joshua Trommel_, Oct 28 2026
